1
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,407
Nobody tells you what it is like the
day after you win.

2
00:00:20,977 --> 00:00:26,819
The stories all stop at the balcony. The crowd
chants, the banner goes up, the panel says

3
00:00:26,839 --> 00:00:32,418
integration complete, and the curtain comes down —
because the interesting part, in a

4
00:00:32,438 --> 00:00:38,214
story, is the getting. Nobody writes the part where
the man who has taken the world sits

5
00:00:38,234 --> 00:00:42,759
down on a Tuesday morning in front of nine
hundred pages about water.

6
00:00:43,329 --> 00:00:49,185
That is what the first years of the Umbrella
Directorate actually were. Water. Grain.

7
00:00:49,205 --> 00:00:55,130
Antibiotics. Power grids in four hundred cities that
had been deferred for forty years

8
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:59,692
by governments that would not be in office
when the bill came due.

9
00:01:01,762 --> 00:01:07,573
He was good at it. That is the part the narrator has
to say plainly, because it is the

10
00:01:07,593 --> 00:01:09,879
reason none of it was ever undone.

11
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:16,224
In the first decade of the Directorate, global
childhood mortality fell by seventy-one

12
00:01:16,244 --> 00:01:22,154
percent. Four hundred million people were connected
to a reliable electricity supply for

13
00:01:22,174 --> 00:01:28,016
the first time. The Sahel water programme, which
nineteen international bodies had been

14
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:33,811
unable to fund for thirty years, was built in six,
because one entity decided to build

15
00:01:33,831 --> 00:01:37,180
it and no committee was permitted
to slow it down.

16
00:01:37,751 --> 00:01:43,758
He did not do it out of compassion. He had said so
from a balcony, in front of six billion

17
00:01:43,778 --> 00:01:49,517
people, and nobody had listened. He did it because a
badly-run world offended him, and

18
00:01:49,537 --> 00:01:55,343
because a population that is fed, watered, and
healthy produces more, resists less, and

19
00:01:55,363 --> 00:02:01,303
— the System was very clear on this point, in panels
he read every morning — pays better.

20
00:02:04,623 --> 00:02:06,367
Assessment. Quarterly.

21
00:02:12,348 --> 00:02:13,993
Nought point four percent.

22
00:02:14,563 --> 00:02:20,572
Thirty-five million people, spread across a planet
of nearly nine billion, who did not

23
00:02:20,592 --> 00:02:26,530
agree. The System counted them every quarter, in a
line of small text, and never once

24
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:28,772
suggested what to do about them.

25
00:02:31,342 --> 00:02:37,891
Doctor Nadia Okafor was sixty-four years old and had
been writing the same book for nineteen

26
00:02:37,911 --> 00:02:38,319
years.

27
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:44,342
She had left Umbrella in twenty twenty-nine, walked
out into the rain without opening

28
00:02:44,362 --> 00:02:49,813
an umbrella, and filed a complaint that had started
everything — the CDC, the Bureau,

29
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:55,542
Carrick, all of it. She had been right about every
single thing in it. She had also been,

30
00:02:55,562 --> 00:03:01,335
from the moment the Court of Chancery wrote footnote
fourteen, a woman that serious people

31
00:03:01,355 --> 00:03:03,974
were careful not to be photographed with.

32
00:03:04,544 --> 00:03:10,570
She had gone home to Lagos in twenty thirty-four. She
had taught virology at a university

33
00:03:10,590 --> 00:03:16,547
for fifteen years, and been an excellent teacher, and
been quietly passed over for every

34
00:03:16,567 --> 00:03:22,320
chair she applied for, because the university's new
science faculty had been built by

35
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:26,328
the Umbrella Foundation and nobody had
to be told anything.

36
00:03:26,897 --> 00:03:32,503
The book was called The Thing In The Basement. It
was four hundred pages long. It was

37
00:03:32,523 --> 00:03:38,327
meticulous, and sourced, and unhysterical, and it
named dates and shipping manifests and

38
00:03:38,347 --> 00:03:43,357
a black binder on a bench on the ninth floor in
February twenty twenty-nine.

39
00:03:43,927 --> 00:03:49,737
Nine publishers read it. Four of them said it was the
most important manuscript they had

40
00:03:49,757 --> 00:03:55,435
ever been sent. None of them published it. Two said
so honestly, over a drink, off the

41
00:03:55,455 --> 00:04:01,132
record, in words that amounted to the same thing: we
would not survive it, and neither

42
00:04:01,152 --> 00:04:01,794
would you.

43
00:04:03,865 --> 00:04:10,005
She published it herself, in twenty fifty-one, as a
file, for nothing, from a server in

44
00:04:10,025 --> 00:04:12,272
a country that no longer exists.

45
00:04:12,842 --> 00:04:18,964
It was downloaded four million times in the first
month. It was never taken down — the

46
00:04:18,984 --> 00:04:25,249
Directorate did not take things down; Tomás's
successors had understood for a generation

47
00:04:25,269 --> 00:04:31,320
that suppression is what makes a document holy. It
was simply surrounded, within nine

48
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:37,534
days, by four hundred thoughtful articles explaining
its context, and by a documentary,

49
00:04:37,554 --> 00:04:43,819
and by the recollections of eleven of her former
colleagues, all of whom spoke about her

50
00:04:43,839 --> 00:04:46,747
with enormous warmth and visible sadness.

51
00:04:47,317 --> 00:04:53,858
By twenty fifty-four, if you searched her name, the
fourth result was a serious, sympathetic,

52
00:04:53,878 --> 00:04:59,856
scrupulously sourced account of a brilliant
scientist whose career had been destroyed

53
00:04:59,876 --> 00:05:00,985
by an obsession.

54
00:05:01,555 --> 00:05:07,645
She lived to ninety-one. She is in every history of
the period. She is always in the same

55
00:05:07,665 --> 00:05:11,834
paragraph, and the paragraph always
contains the word tragic.

56
00:05:14,403 --> 00:05:16,643
He caught himself waiting for an answer.

57
00:05:17,213 --> 00:05:22,887
There was none to have. He had asked the question out
of habit — what do we do about the

58
00:05:22,907 --> 00:05:28,518
woman in Lagos — and had noticed, four seconds
later, that there had been nobody to ask

59
00:05:28,538 --> 00:05:29,424
for two years.

60
00:05:29,994 --> 00:05:34,178
He did nothing about the woman in Lagos.
He had never needed to.

61
00:05:37,247 --> 00:05:43,361
Tomás Reyes stopped taking the therapy in the spring of
twenty fifty-eight, and told nobody,

62
00:05:43,381 --> 00:05:46,295
and it was four years before
anyone noticed.

63
00:05:46,865 --> 00:05:52,817
He was sixty. He had been chief narrative officer
for twenty-six years, and then, from

64
00:05:52,837 --> 00:05:58,860
twenty forty-nine, Director of Public Understanding
for the Umbrella Directorate, which

65
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:02,333
was the same job with a title that
made him tired.

66
00:06:02,903 --> 00:06:08,656
He had shaped what nine billion people believed for a
generation. Everything he had ever

67
00:06:08,676 --> 00:06:14,363
put out had been true — that had been his rule since
twenty thirty, and he had kept it,

68
00:06:14,383 --> 00:06:20,268
and it had cost him more than lying would have. He had
simply chosen, four thousand times,

69
00:06:20,288 --> 00:06:23,810
which true things nine billion
people would see first.

70
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:31,735
He still played the break-room clip. He had played it,
by his own count, forty-one thousand

71
00:06:31,755 --> 00:06:32,122
times.

72
00:06:32,692 --> 00:06:38,405
In twenty sixty-two, when he was sixty-four and
visibly ageing among colleagues who were

73
00:06:38,425 --> 00:06:41,923
not, the Director came to the house
on the Brandywine.

74
00:06:44,112 --> 00:06:44,966
You've stopped.

75
00:06:45,487 --> 00:06:46,448
Four years ago.

76
00:06:46,968 --> 00:06:47,545
Why?

77
00:06:50,064 --> 00:06:54,965
Because I'm the only one left who was in the room, Doc.
Dana's gone. Walter's gone. Evelyn's

78
00:06:54,985 --> 00:06:59,619
— Evelyn's whatever Evelyn is now. Catherine's in
Ireland refusing to answer her phone.

79
00:06:59,639 --> 00:07:04,165
And I look around a table and every single person
on it has been made by us, and none

80
00:07:04,185 --> 00:07:07,535
of them has ever been in a room where we
couldn't make payroll.

81
00:07:08,935 --> 00:07:14,503
I've spent thirty-six years explaining you to the
human race. I'd quite like to stop before

82
00:07:14,523 --> 00:07:18,556
I stop being able to tell whether I'm
explaining or just — saying.

83
00:07:21,076 --> 00:07:27,433
The Director sat with him on the balcony for a long
time. It was the only recorded instance

84
00:07:27,453 --> 00:07:33,391
of him cancelling an Accord Council session, other
than one, sixty-seven years later,

85
00:07:33,411 --> 00:07:34,933
in the Keel of Arklay.

86
00:07:35,622 --> 00:07:40,930
You could have asked me for anything. In thirty-six
years you never asked me for one thing.

87
00:07:41,449 --> 00:07:45,124
I know. That's how I knew I still worked for
you and not the other thing.

88
00:07:48,144 --> 00:07:54,259
Tomás Reyes died in twenty sixty-six, at
sixty-eight, in the house on the Brandywine,

89
00:07:54,279 --> 00:07:55,631
with the sound off.

90
00:07:56,201 --> 00:08:02,649
He left instructions that there was to be no public
observance of any kind, and the Directorate

91
00:08:02,669 --> 00:08:08,572
honoured them exactly, which was itself the most
enormous public observance imaginable:

92
00:08:08,592 --> 00:08:14,496
nine billion people noticed, on the same afternoon,
that nothing at all was being said.

93
00:08:17,565 --> 00:08:23,605
There was opposition. It is important to record what
happened to it, because what happened

94
00:08:23,625 --> 00:08:25,153
to it was not violence.

95
00:08:25,723 --> 00:08:32,250
In twenty fifty-five a coalition of eleven national
parliaments — the last eleven in which

96
00:08:32,270 --> 00:08:38,797
a genuine opposition still sat — proposed the
Sovereignty Restoration Compact, which would

97
00:08:38,817 --> 00:08:45,053
have withdrawn its signatories from Article Nine and
rebuilt independent manufacturing

98
00:08:45,073 --> 00:08:47,016
capacity over thirty years.

99
00:08:47,586 --> 00:08:53,654
It was a serious proposal, written by serious people,
and the Directorate's response was

100
00:08:53,674 --> 00:08:56,490
the most sophisticated thing it ever did.

101
00:08:57,059 --> 00:08:58,567
It published the numbers.

102
00:08:59,136 --> 00:09:05,073
Every one of them. The complete cost of rebuilding
parallel pharmaceutical capacity in

103
00:09:05,093 --> 00:09:11,237
eleven countries — honestly costed, by the only
organisation on Earth that actually knew,

104
00:09:11,257 --> 00:09:17,124
with no exaggeration whatsoever, because
exaggeration would have been catastrophic if

105
00:09:17,144 --> 00:09:23,704
caught. The projected interruption in supply. The
projected excess mortality during transition,

106
00:09:23,724 --> 00:09:29,729
by country, by age band, with the methodology
attached in full for any expert to check.

107
00:09:30,301 --> 00:09:36,568
The number for the eleven countries, over thirty years,
was four hundred thousand additional

108
00:09:36,588 --> 00:09:42,718
deaths. It was accurate. Four independent academies
checked it, including two that loathed

109
00:09:42,738 --> 00:09:46,546
Umbrella, and all four confirmed it
within nine percent.

110
00:09:48,615 --> 00:09:54,052
The Directorate did not campaign against the
Compact. It published the figure, offered

111
00:09:54,072 --> 00:09:59,447
to fund the transition itself if the parliaments
voted for it, and said nothing else.

112
00:10:00,018 --> 00:10:06,399
Nine of the eleven parliaments voted it down within
a year. The two that passed it held

113
00:10:06,419 --> 00:10:13,316
referendums, as their constitutions required. Both
referendums failed, by margins of sixty-one

114
00:10:13,336 --> 00:10:15,156
and seventy-four percent.

115
00:10:17,346 --> 00:10:19,899
You did not even have to lie.

116
00:10:20,419 --> 00:10:26,237
He had not thought that. It had simply arrived, in
his own voice, in a room on the fourth

117
00:10:26,257 --> 00:10:31,813
floor with no name on the door, and he had noticed
it arriving, and had not been able

118
00:10:31,833 --> 00:10:34,764
to decide whether it was a
memory or a habit.

119
00:10:37,835 --> 00:10:43,370
In twenty sixty-one, the first human beings who had
been born after the Accord turned

120
00:10:43,390 --> 00:10:43,959
fourteen.

121
00:10:44,528 --> 00:10:50,476
They had never lived in a world with more than one
answer in it. They had never seen an

122
00:10:50,496 --> 00:10:56,513
election that decided anything, or a shortage, or a
war, or a pandemic, or a border that

123
00:10:56,533 --> 00:11:02,412
mattered. They were healthier, taller, better
educated, and by every instrument anyone

124
00:11:02,432 --> 00:11:07,350
could devise, happier than any generation in
the history of the species.

125
00:11:07,921 --> 00:11:14,422
The Directorate's own longitudinal study — four
hundred thousand subjects, run honestly,

126
00:11:14,442 --> 00:11:20,870
published in full — found one statistically
significant anomaly in that cohort, and the

127
00:11:20,890 --> 00:11:26,428
Director read the paper himself, four times,
on the night it was published.

128
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,938
Under stress, when asked to make a decision in
conditions of genuine uncertainty, the

129
00:11:32,958 --> 00:11:38,896
cohort's response time was markedly slower than
every preceding generation's, and its

130
00:11:38,916 --> 00:11:40,719
confidence markedly lower.

131
00:11:41,288 --> 00:11:47,489
The paper's authors — who were Umbrella scientists, and
who published it because the Director

132
00:11:47,509 --> 00:11:53,243
had personally established in twenty forty-nine that
Directorate science would publish

133
00:11:53,263 --> 00:11:59,197
everything or it would not be science — offered four
possible explanations, and concluded

134
00:11:59,217 --> 00:12:00,937
that more work was needed.

135
00:12:01,508 --> 00:12:06,937
He knew exactly what it was. He had built it
deliberately, over thirty-four years, and

136
00:12:06,957 --> 00:12:11,562
it had worked, and a research group had just
measured it in the children.

137
00:12:14,131 --> 00:12:18,578
He read the paper four times, and then he
sat in the dark for a while.

138
00:12:19,149 --> 00:12:25,042
There was a version of this story in which he did
something about that finding. The narrator

139
00:12:25,062 --> 00:12:30,506
has looked for it, in four hundred years of
Directorate records, and it is not there.

140
00:12:30,526 --> 00:12:36,034
He funded the follow-up study generously. He read
every one of them for the next three

141
00:12:36,054 --> 00:12:38,991
centuries. He never once acted
on any of them.

142
00:12:39,561 --> 00:12:45,534
Because the alternative was to reintroduce,
deliberately, into a species that had finally

143
00:12:45,554 --> 00:12:51,661
stopped fighting itself, the thing that had made it
fight: disagreement. Real disagreement,

144
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:57,587
with real stakes, and real mistakes, and real
consequences, and a hundred million people

145
00:12:57,607 --> 00:13:03,378
dead somewhere down the line of it, because that is
what disagreement costs and always

146
00:13:03,398 --> 00:13:03,648
has.

147
00:13:04,217 --> 00:13:10,107
He had done the arithmetic. He always did the
arithmetic. It came out the same way every

148
00:13:10,127 --> 00:13:12,793
time, for three hundred and sixty years.

149
00:13:15,363 --> 00:13:20,730
Evelyn Marsh had once told him she was the only
person in the building who had actually

150
00:13:20,750 --> 00:13:24,632
counted. She had been wrong about that.
There were two of them.
